part.text:
`requestMatchers(String...)` and `requestMatchers(HttpMethod, String...)` exist. In Security 6.1, `requestMatchers(String...)` — what matcher does it create? AbstractRequestMatcherRegistry in 6.1: it checks if MVC is present (HandlerMappingIntrospector) — if MVC is on the classpath, it uses `MvcRequestMatcher`... Actually in 6.1: `requestMatchers(String...)` creates `AntPathRequestMatcher` if no MVC, else `MvcRequestMatcher`. Hmm — hmm, there was a security advisory (CVE-2023-34062?) hmm. In Spring Security 6.1.x, `AbstractRequestMatcherRegistry.requestMatchers(String... patterns)`:

```java
public C requestMatchers(String... patterns) {
    return chainRequestMatchers(Arrays.stream(patterns)
        .map(pattern -> requestMatcher(pattern))...);
}
```

Hmm — 6.1 introduced `RequestMatcherFactory`?? Hmm. Actually I remember in 6.1, if spring-mvc is present, requestMatchers(String) → MvcRequestMatcher (with HandlerMappingIntrospector); otherwise AntPathRequestMatcher. And in 6.5/6.4?? they moved to PathPatternRequestMatcher by default in 7.0/6.5? Whatever — for our purposes, both AntPathRequestMatcher and MvcRequestMatcher support the ant pattern `{js,html}` syntax... hmm — MvcRequestMatcher delegates pattern matching to the MVC path matcher — which with ant_path_matcher (our configured strategy) supports {js,html}.

Hmm wait — but there's a subtlety with MvcRequestMatcher in 6.1 + our `ant_path_matcher` MVC config: fine.

But hmm — there's the known issue: Spring Security 6.1's requestMatchers(String) creating MvcRequestMatcher requires the `HandlerMappingIntrospector` bean — it's present in Spring MVC apps. OK.

Actually — simpler and more deterministic: keep the patterns identical to the original (they're all plain ant patterns; "/app/**/*.{js,html}" uses the brace alternation which AntPathMatcher supports).

